From e5505a6128dea142a965afdfdee3163af93a745e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 12:10:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban GC: optional sub-day
 kanban.worktree_retention_hours

hermes-worker-2 keeps 110-165 GiB of terminal scratch trees under
kanban.worktree_retention_days: 1, because the days key accepts whole
days only and the pool runs ~250-300 card runs/day at ~0.4 GiB each.
The pool acceptance (t_01a1c82e4f774d5f) is < 15 GiB.

Add an optional kanban.worktree_retention_hours (a non-negative number).
When set, retention_days() returns hours / 24 and it takes precedence
over worktree_retention_days; when unset (or null) behaviour is exactly
as before, including the int return. An invalid hours value (negative,
bool, string, NaN/inf, list/map) disables age-based GC with the same
warning as an invalid days key; it never falls back to days. 0 disables.
An explicit `kanban gc --worktree-retention-days N` still wins.

Only the window changes. Every guard is untouched: terminal-owner
recheck, owner_unverified / eviction custody fail-closed, live
owner-tagged job protection, archive-before-remove. The callers
(gc_task_scratch, gc_custody, gc_task_worktrees, _sweep_repo) already
use the value only as `days * 86400` and `days <= 0`, so their
annotations widen from int to float.

Reverse: remove the hours key, which falls back to days.

Proof:
- tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_gc_retention_hours.py: 21 tests
  (precedence, null = unset, zero, 9 invalid values, a 3 h cut-off that
  archives a 4 h-old clone tree and keeps a 2 h-old one, the pending
  eligible_at log, days-only cut-off unchanged, invalid hours keeps a
  10-day-old tree, explicit CLI days wins). RED on base: 16 failed /
  5 passed (the 5 are the unchanged-behaviour controls). GREEN: 21/21.
- Every test file importing the GC/custody module or retention_days:
  20 files, 442 passed.
- scripts/check --base origin/main: 19 checks ok.
